regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_deepseek.txt:17:Yes, that is right. The `default:` arm still diagnoses and exits for both reads and writes; `LCA_IOC_STAT0` and `LCA_IOC_STAT1` have no case; and explicit exits remain in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa)`, and the interrupt assert/deassert functions. Nothing else from the packet.
regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\grok.txt:23:1. **`#447` refused A where the rejected bits *were* the consumed feature.** I went past the packet to the actual RCR1 arm in `dev_sh4.c`. The shipped fix is not “move a whitelist below the store.” It is: if `CIE|AIE` are set, decline the whole write; **every other value still stores.** The refused bits are the unimplemented interrupt enables in an otherwise-live register. LCA’s window guards are the opposite shape: equality against one (or two) observed firmware constants, with `TODO: Actually implement this` on the same arm. Premise 5 is true as history and **false as a transfer**. Applying RCR1’s edit here is warrant-transfer without the warrant.
