regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:81:  A. Switch 1's class labels. Mutants: change a class label (e.g., case 4 → case 12?). Then class 4 becomes unimplemented → fallback halts class-4 faults → every class-4 row (misalign, umisalign, error...) RED. Caught. Swap labels between classes? E.g., case 4 ↔ case 5? Then a MISALIGNED fault (vector 4) hits... case 4 is now case 5's body? Hmm, if the arms' bodies are identical (`implemented = 1`), swapping labels does nothing observable except... they're all just `implemented = 1`? Presumably each arm sets implemented = 1. If the bodies are identical, label swaps are equivalent mutants — not distinguishable, not a breach (mutant must CHANGE behavior to be a kill-worthy mutant; an equivalent mutant isn't a clean sweep, it's equivalent).
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:517:  And Q2: which of the ten dispositions is wrong? My answer: the trapfrozen10 row closure — not the row itself (it does kill M_ERRORFRZOFF and M_SFRZTRAP10 as measured), but the disposition that codex's pass-6 finding is CLOSED by it. The finding was that the ERROR arm's eligibility predicate was unswept-pinned; the repair pins only the SFRZ-set polarity, so the cell's twin (`implemented = (PSR & SFRZ)`, and more broadly the arm's {PSR bit} surface) still sweeps. The docstring line "switch 1's ERROR eligibility under SFRZ by trapfrozen10's HALT/DELIVER" is true only of the `!(PSR & SFRZ)` polarity. Everything else (the other four rows, refatal, the withdrawal, the gate repair, the interval, 0..3) checks out.
