regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_20260908\grok.txt:139:**Same-function precedent, before house style at large:** the two `T_BASE` arms in this switch already store the written value, leave a `TODO: Actually implement this`, and do not kill. Window translation is unimplemented for the *accepted* 8 MB / 1 GB encodings too. The six value guards are the outliers in this switch, not the idiom. `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` ignore. `struct lca_data` already has backing storage for all eight window/CONF/TB fields, and every one of these arms **stores before it kills** — so “decline” that keeps the store is an accept with a complaint.
regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_20260908\grok.txt:148:| `W_MASK0/1`, other `<31:20>` encodings | **decline with a once-only announcement** (optional: still store the field, since “unsupported” is not MBZ) | `:9578` is the only closed “unsupported” sentence in this chapter |
regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_20260908\grok.txt:153:A `(once)` on every legal 16 MB / 1 MB / 8 MB-on-window-1 write would treat a documented size as a diagnostic event, and would also fire on Linux’s own init if aimed at “windows are not translated.” If a DMA-unimplemented announcement is wanted, latch it once on first `WEN=1`, not on value mismatch. Do not decline a legal write.
regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_20260908\grok.txt:161:`device_add(machine, "lca")` is `ST_ALPHABOOK1` only (`machine_alpha.c:57-61`). The IOC is mapped at `0x180000000`, reachable from a guest via K0SEG (`0xfffffc0000000000 | phys`) with an ordinary `stq`. `regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py` already does that on this machine (it even names `LCA_IOC_CONF` at `K0SEG|0x180000020`).
regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_20260908\grok.txt:171:**I could not find a shipped guest in this tree that would hit these six arms.** Linux `core_lca.c` (and the NetBSD header this file vendor-copied) programs exactly the four accepted window constants and `TB_ENA = 0x80`. Those writes **pass** the value guards. A Linux LCA boot would then die on a *different* arm: `IOC_PAR_DIS` at `+0x0E0` and `IOC_STAT0` at `+0x040` have no `case` and hit `default: … exit(1)`. GXemul does not run SROM, so the documented write-zero-to-both-bases sequence is not issued for free. AlphaBook1 interrupt handlers are still `fatal`+`exit(1)`, so a real OS dies there even if the windows were the accepted constants.
