regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:67:  - `writeflag == MEM_WRITE` -> `writeflag != MEM_WRITE` (inverted direction): then byte writes declined, wide writes land. Byte-write decline: `acc all` (twelve byte stores, zero declines) would red at any arm — covered by GROW's channel (A/C). Wide-write land: same as FALL/DEL — covered by arm channels. Actually `!=` is one token... hmm, is it? `==` -> `!=` is a plausible typo-class edit. The acc all row catches the byte side; the arm readback rows catch the wide-land side. So arguably covered by existing channels even though not in the shape list.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:68:  - `len != 1` -> `len == 1` (inverted): declines byte writes (acc all reds), lets wide writes land (arm readbacks red). Covered similarly.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:69:  - `len != 1` -> `len != 2`: declines halfword (all the put h / st.h rows red), lets byte and word land. Word landing silently — `latch all w` would drop from 12 to 11 (word lands without announce). Covered.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:70:  - `len != 1` -> `len < 1` or `len > 1`... `len > 1` declines everything wide including len 2,4,8 — same as `!= 1` for present rows; byte lands. Effectively equivalent for the rows. `len < 1` never declines — same as DEL, covered.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:72:  - RDDECL is `==` -> `<=`. What about `writeflag == MEM_WRITE` -> `writeflag >= MEM_READ`? Equivalent to `<=` inversion... `>= MEM_READ` is always true (MEM_READ=0, MEM_WRITE=1), so guard always fires on... wait no. Guard is `len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE`. Changing the second conjunct to `writeflag >= MEM_READ` makes it always true when len != 1 — i.e., declines wide reads AND wide writes. Wide reads declined: hrd/wrd nod rows red. Same channel as RDDECL. Covered.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:73:  - Changing `len != 1` to `len != 8`? Declines only 8-byte... `put d` — is there a put d row? The premise says `put w` / `put d` give len 4/8. latch all w uses put w (len 4). There's no `put d`/8-byte-wide row in the 96. A `len != 8` guard would decline only len-8 writes; halfword and word writes would LAND silently. That's like DEL for h and w. Arm readbacks (st.h/put h) red; latch all w reds (word lands silently, count drops). So covered by existing channels.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:75:    But wait — what about a guard `len != 1` -> `len != 4`? Declines word writes only; halfword lands silently. Arm readback rows (halfword) red. Covered.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:85:    Also: what about the announce text shape — the name string. Disclosed: "five odd-arm `dcl' rows count the generic decline text, so a change of the NAME STRING alone at one of them is invisible". Disclosed, fine.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:87:    Another shape: `break;` -> `return 0;`? In the guard: `pcc2_width_decline(...); break;` — if `break` becomes `return`, the function returns instead of breaking out of the switch — for the device access function, returning skips the rest. Effect: same as break for the arm (the write is declined, function exits). Hmm, actually what comes after the switch? If there's post-switch code (e.g., a return of the value or reassert), `return` might skip it. For a WRITE decline the observable difference... rows check announce count and readback value. If post-switch code matters (like updating something), could differ. This is speculative without reading the actual file. The `FALL` shape covers break removal; `break` -> `return` is a different one-token-ish edit. Would rows catch it? If devaccess_pcc2's post-switch code is just `return 1;` then return 0 vs break-then-return-1 might differ in return value semantics (0 = access failed?). That could cause a guest exception... Actually in gxemul, memory_rw device handlers return 0 or 1 (or -1?). If the decline path returned 0 instead of 1, the access would be treated as failed → maybe a machine exception for the guest rows, or for the operator path... This could break survival rows, so probably caught. Not the cleanest counterexample.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:91:    `len != 1` -> `len <= 1`? For len=1: 1<=1 true → guard fires?? wait guard is "if (len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE) decline". If it becomes `len <= 1`, byte writes declined → acc all reds. Covered by GROW channel.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:103:    Now the SWAP shape: guard operand `len` -> `idata > 0xff`. Marked M at GENCTL (rb genctl lo kills it: st.h 0x0022 lands 0x22 ≠ 0x11) and C elsewhere: "latch all, its value 0x0003 fits a byte, so a value-test guard at ANY arm lets it land and drops the count". Check: with SWAP at arm X, `put h 0x0003` at X: idata = 0x0003, `idata > 0xff` false → guard doesn't fire → no decline announce → the write lands. latch all count drops from 12 to 11. Caught. Also the write lands — does it matter? For VECBASE: stores 0x03 & 0xf0 = 0x00, prints HUH (idata & ~0xf0 → 0x03 & ~0xf0 = 3 → HUH print). Count of DECL drops → caught. OK.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:105:    But subtle: SWAP as written is `len != 1` -> `idata > 0xff`. The full guard is `len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` -> `idata > 0xff && writeflag == MEM_WRITE`. What if the swap is `len != 1` -> `idata != 0`? A wide write of 0x0000 would land silently. latch all uses 0x0003 (nonzero) → guard fires → decline → count stays 12. Arm readbacks use 0xaab8/0xaabb (nonzero) → declined → green. So `idata != 0` survives! Is that in the contract? SWAP is named as one specific shape (`idata > 0xff`), and the C warrant is "its value 0x0003 fits a byte, so a value-test guard at ANY arm lets it land and drops the count" — that reasoning covers value-tests that 0x0003 PASSES (i.e., tests that accept 0x0003: `> 0xff` accepts it... wait no. `idata > 0xff` with idata=0x0003 is FALSE → guard does NOT fire → write lands → count drops. Yes so the C channel works for value tests under which 0x0003 "fits"/is accepted. For `idata != 0`, 0x0003 is nonzero → guard FIRES → declined → count stays 12. So latch all does NOT cover `idata != 0`. Does any other row? The wide values used in readback rows are 0xaab8, 0xaabb, 0x0303, 0x0022, 0x1234 — all nonzero. A wide write of value 0 anywhere? `wwr sccerr` uses r6=0x00000000! That's a word write of 0 to SCCERR: with `idata != 0` guard at SCCERR: guard false → write lands → stores 0 (SCCERR arm stores 0 anyway) → wwr sccerr survives (exit0) and wwr sccerr lv green, and "wwr sccerr dcl" — wait is there a wwr sccerr dcl row? The gate list includes "wwr sccerr dcl" — hmm, looking at the old gate list: "wwr sccerr dcl" is in the list. And the new list also has "wwr sccerr dcl" and separately "hwr sccerr dcl" was added. So wwr sccerr dcl expects DECL count 1 for a word write of 0x00000000 to SCCERR. With `idata != 0` guard at SCCERR: guard doesn't fire → no decline → count 0 ≠ 1 → RED. Caught at SCCERR! But at the other eleven blocks, no row wide-writes a zero value. `idata != 0` at, say, MASK: all wide writes to MASK in the probe are 0xaabb (op mask) and 0x0003 (latch all) and 0x00000003 (latch all w) — all nonzero → all declined → green. acc all stores bytes → guard `idata != 0` — wait, byte writes: len==1, guard is `idata != 0 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` — the len conjunct is GONE (replaced). So byte writes with nonzero idata would be DECLINED! acc all uses r5=0x10 (nonzero) for all twelve blocks → at a swapped arm, the byte store announces a decline → acc all reds. CAUGHT. Hmm. So `idata != 0` reds acc all at any arm (since the guard no longer excludes len==1). Right — SWAP removes the len conjunct entirely, so ANY byte write with a value failing the test is declined. acc all uses 0x10 everywhere → any value-test that 0x10 passes... for `idata != 0`: 0x10 != 0 → guard fires → decline announced → acc all count becomes ≥1 ≠ 0 → red. OK so caught. Good — so SWAP variants are generally caught via acc all (byte side) or latch all (wide side), as long as either 0x10 passes the test or 0x0003 passes the test. A test where BOTH 0x10 and 0x0003 are accepted... e.g., `idata > 0xff`: 0x10 > 0xff false (byte lands, acc all green), 0x0003 > 0xff false (wide lands, latch all reds). Covered — that's exactly the measured one. A test like `idata == 0x10`? Byte store of 0x10 declined at that arm → acc all reds. `idata < 0x10`? 0x10 < 0x10 false → byte lands; 0x0003 < 0x10 true → declined → latch all green; wide values 0xaab8 → 0xaab8 < 0x10 false → LANDS → arm readback reds (e.g., rb genctl expects 0x50 unchanged... wait GENCTL pre-set 0x50, st.h 0xaabb lands 0xbb → red). Covered. So the SWAP family seems genuinely closed by the combination. Good.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:107:    Now GROW: `len != 1` -> `len != 0` declines bytes too → acc all reds. Variant: `len != 1` -> `len >= 1` (declines everything incl. bytes) → acc all reds. `len != 1` -> `1` (always decline) → acc all reds. Covered.
