he fall. Wait order: body = store masked; if (idata & ~0xf0) HUH. For 0x50: no HUH. Then (no break) → T1CMP case: memcpy for writeflag==MEM_WRITE && relative_addr+len <= PCC2_SIZE → reg[3] = 0x10/0x50. Value-identical. SO: deleting the final `break;` of the VECBASE arm passes all 96 rows! Property broken: out-of-contract — the arm now ALSO executes the counter memcpy (a raw byte store, and for a len-1 write it's benign-identical, but the property "each arm does exactly its body" is violated; for other values it would corrupt: e.g., st.b 0x5f → stores 0x50 then memcpy overwrites 0x5f — the DECLINE/mask semantics of byte writes broken). Is this "in contract"? The enumeration's shapes are guard-internal (DEL/LEN/LEN2/KILL/ALIAS/FALL/SWAP/GROW/RDDECL). FALL is "guard kept, announce kept, break removed" — the GUARD's break. The arm-TERMINAL break removal is a ninth... tenth shape — control flow at the arm level, not the guard level. For VECBASE it's value-invisible to every row (no row byte-writes an off-mask value to VECBASE). Hmm — but wait, does any row catch it? "acc genctl nod" style rows count DECL==0 for byte writes — fall-through memcpy doesn't print. rb vecbase expects 0x50 ✓. So yes — passes 96/96. Is the broken property inside the contract's CATCHES? The contract catches guard defeats; arm-terminal fall-through corrupting register state on LEGAL byte writes is... "the de-escalation clause: byte writes at the LEGAL width at every reachable arm still land, read back through each arm's own mask" — with VECBASE's break removed, a byte write lands as the RAW byte, not the masked byte — but rows only use mask-fitting values, so "read back through each arm's own mask" is untested for off-mask bytes. Hmm, is writing 0x5f and reading back 0x50 (mask semantics) part of the contract? "read back through each arm's own mask" — the phrase suggests readback expectations respect the mask. acc rows: do per-arm acc rows verify readback values? acc genctl/acc t2ctl/etc. — for VE