regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:996:  - SCCERR FALL = E: verified from source — arm stores 0 unconditionally (line 550), no print, and the only stores to pcctwo_reg are within each keyed case; nothing stores nonzero SCCERR. FALL vs decline differ only in storing 0 to a cell that's always 0. E holds. One caveat: the E warrant says "grep SCCERR dev_pcc2.c" — the store is via `relative_addr`, so the grep alone doesn't prove it; I verified by case-keying. The warrant is TRUE but its stated deriver (grep) is insufficient — a store `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr] = X` inside `case PCCTWO_SCCERR` doesn't mention SCCERR literally. Nit: the deriver named is weaker than the actual proof.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1020:  One more adversarial thought on the RDDECL closure: the nod rows pin `writeflag <= MEM_WRITE` (declines reads). But a DIFFERENT direction-split defeat: `writeflag == MEM_WRITE` → `writeflag == MEM_READ`?? That declines wide READS only (writes land). Covered: hrd nod reds (decline printed) + arm rb rows red (wide writes land). What about dropping the conjunct entirely (`len != 1` alone — decline wide reads AND writes)? hrd nod reds ✓. What about moving the writeflag test to the body instead of the guard? Behaviorally identical. OK.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1028:  And: `pcc2_width_decline(d, PCC2_W_X_LEN, "PCCTWO_X", len)` — swap the NAME strings of two blocks? Five odd-arm dcl rows count generic DECL → invisible at those five (disclosed). At named-text arms (genctl via "rb genctl dcl"? — wait which rows count NAMED text? "rb t2ctl dcl" counts "write to PCCTWO_TxCTL " + DECL — named. The odd-arm `op X dcl` rows count generic DECL. A name swap between two odd blocks (e.g., SCCICR↔SCCRX strings): op dcl counts generic → both still 1 → latch all still 12 → invisible. Disclosed ("a change of the NAME STRING alone at one of them is invisible"). ✓ consistent.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1036:  Edit: in pcc2_width_decline, `d->warned |= (1u << bit)` → `d->warned = (1u << bit)`.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1047:  - acc all / nod rows (DECL==0): no wide writes at all... wait acc all is byte writes — no declines either way ✓ 0.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1051:  So yes: 96/96 under `|=`→`=`. And the broken property is real: a driver session doing GENCTL, VECBASE, GENCTL gets TWO "GENCTL declined" announces — the "(once)" promise broken. The contract CATCHES bullet claims this exact failure ("dropped `|=' floods") is caught via "same site twice must announce ONCE" — false as a channel for THIS edit, because the flood needs an intervening block. CONFIRMED escape.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1059:  Third: the len-8 lane — no row ever drives len 8 at the twelve (put d/ld.d absent), so the landing closure is over {2,4}; a multi-token guard landing only 8 would pass. One-token? `len != 1` → `len != 8` declines bytes too (acc all reds). `len != 1` → `len < 8`... declines 1,2,4: acc all reds. So no ONE-token len-8 escape; worth one line as a named residual (denominator honesty).
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1065:  LEN S cells: LEN lands halfword, declines word. At arms where FALL or DEL measured the halfword landing channel (all twelve have one: rb rows, noig rows, sccerr message rows), LEN's halfword-landing half is the same landing → red. ✓ Also declines words → latch all w stays 12 (word declined → announced → 12 ✓ green) — consistent, doesn't matter, rb rows red.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass4_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:1075:  GROW A/C: measured at PSCALEADJ (3c, acc all red). For per-arm-acc blocks: acc X nod expects 0 declines in the byte session → GROW at that arm announces → red. ✓
