regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:143:  Also for Q2/Q3 I should grep the fork's idiom for reconciling independent mode fields — e.g., look for existing "decline" patterns with latched announce (SGIRE_W_*), which is clearly the house idiom. Already visible: #497-#500, sgirandompixel all use latched decline. So disposition 4 matches house idiom. But brief asks to grep for precedent of reconciling two independent guest-chosen mode fields. Could grep for clamp patterns. I'll do a light pass.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:296:  Q3 (option-set completeness + fork idiom): house idiom is latched decline (SGIRE_W_* bits, "declined (once)"). Disposition 4 matches it. Any missing member? Possible additional dispositions:
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:300:  Also: is there an in-tree precedent for reconciling two independent guest-chosen mode fields? The MTE code itself: `if (depth != 8) fatal+exit` on the LINEAR path (an upstream refuse), and draw_primitive's `step_x != src_bufdepth` check (fatal, continues) — a cross-check precedent between two guest fields (step_x vs src_bufdepth)! That's exactly an in-tree precedent of reconciling two independent guest-chosen fields, and it chooses "announce and continue" (declined-ish, unlatched). Also dev_sgi_gbe's len clamp (OB-14) is clamp idiom. I should grep for others quickly. The fork's dominant recent idiom (#497-#500, sgirandompixel) is latched decline — disposition 4. Good.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:650:     - The #497 width guards — decline idiom (latched).
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:651:     - dev_mec... Let me do a quick grep for pairs of fields compared. Honestly, the honest answer: the fork's dominant recent idiom for "guest-chosen combination we don't model" is the latched decline (#497–#500, sgirandompixel). There is no in-tree precedent that RECONCILES (pads/clamps) two independent mode fields into agreement; the precedents are decline (#497+), clamp-to-buffer (OB-14, a length not a mode pair), and announce-and-continue (step_x). So option 4 (decline the mismatched pair, latched) is the idiom-matching disposition; option 1 (memset sizeof) is the minimal-UB fix with defined observable behaviour (zeroes). The brief's own note that the project has previously taken "pick the one that is not UB and say so" applies.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:655:  6. Q4: witness design. 0x1020 for PATH B: sufficient? Reachability — LIN_A forced valid ✓. Oracle: brief's known-stale-content proposal. My analysis above: for PATH B, the paint step should be a GET at 0x1220 (depth 4) from known memory — that fills buf[0..3] with KNOWN bytes — then GET at 0x1020 from different known memory; *color = newbuf0<<24 | old1<<16 | old2<<8 | old3; caller PUTs it to dst at 0x1220; read dst and assert bytes 1..3 == source1 bytes 1..3 (which dst2's own memory does not hold). Sound, modulo frame reuse. Can carry-over be made architectural? Within one draw_primitive RECTANGLE loop with XFER_EN, the GET (line 860) and PUT (line 906) happen per pixel — two different call sites but the same depth. For the two-GET sequence the probe drives via guest stores; each pixel's GET is a separate call from the same call site → same frame. The reuse is a property of the C abstract machine? No — it's not guaranteed by the standard, but it IS deterministic for a fixed binary: same call site, same SP, no intervening calls that clobber those 8 bytes... wait, there ARE intervening calls: memory_rw (called BY horrible_getputpixel itself, deeper — its frame is below buf, doesn't touch buf), and between the two horrible_getputpixel calls, the loop body runs (no other calls except the PUT's horrible_getputpixel at 906 if draw... in XFER rectangle loop: GET(860) → PUT(906) → next iteration GET(860). The PUT call at 906 REUSES THE SAME FRAME (same call depth, same caller) — and the PUT writes buf[0..3] with the colour! So between GET(pixel N) and GET(pixel N+1), a PUT happens at the same stack slot, overwriting buf[0..3] with the PUT's encoding of *color. So the stale bytes at GET N+1's buf[1..3] are NOT pixel-N's GET bytes — they're pixel-N's PUT bytes (the colour just written, re-encoded per dst_mode). Hmm! That changes the oracle design: paint pixel 1 = PUT at 0x1320? No wait — for PATH B the sequence is: GET(src1, 0x1020) then PUT(dst1, 0x1220)... the XFER loop does GET(src_mode) then PUT(dst_mode) per pixel. The probe controls src_mode and dst_mode separately! So set src_mode = 0x1020 (depth 1, RGBA) and dst_mode = 0x1220. Then per pixel: GET reads 1 byte from src, combines with stale buf[1..3]; PUT writes the combined colour to dst. The stale buf[1..3] at GET time for pixel N+1 = the bytes the PUT of pixel N left in buf[0..3] = the RGBA encoding of the colour written to dst[N]. Which is KNOWN if we know what was written — but what was written depends on the stale bytes of the previous GET... induction. For pixel 0: buf state unknown (first call). Pixel 0's GET: color = src0_byte0<<24 | X1<<16 | X2<<8 | X3 (X unknown). PUT pixel 0: buf[0..3] = that colour's bytes = {src0_b0, X1, X2, X3}. Pixel 1's GET: color = src1_b0<<24 | X1<<16 | X2<<8 | X3 — the SAME X bytes. So from pixel 1 onward the unknown X is stable — dst[1] and dst[2] share the same low 24 bits in their upper... wait: color = (b0<<24)+(X1<<16)+(X2<<8)+X3, PUT RGBA encodes buf={b0,X1,X2,X3} — wait buf[0]=*color>>24 = b0, buf[1]=X1, etc. So dst pixel N (N≥1) = {srcN_b0, X1, X2, X3}: the low three bytes CONSTANT across pixels and equal to whatever the first frame held. The oracle: write src memory so that src pixel bytes differ (b0 varies per pixel), and assert dst pixels 1..N all share IDENTICAL low-24-bits — which a correct implementation (reading 4 real bytes per pixel, or zero-filling) would NOT produce (real src memory has varying bytes at positions 1..3 of each pixel; zero-fill gives 0). Hmm — but a memset fix gives low24 = 0; correct hardware gives the real src bytes; the bug gives constant X. Three distinguishable outcomes IF X ≠ 0 and X ≠ real bytes. X is first-frame stack garbage — could be 0 by luck! Control: also assert the value is NOT what the fixed build produces... circular. Better control: BEFORE the XFER, do a paint pass that leaves KNOWN bytes in buf[0..3]: e.g., a PUT at 0x1220 (matched) of a distinctive colour C — that leaves buf[0..3] = C's bytes at that stack slot. Then the XFER GETs at 0x1020 inherit X = C's bytes 1..3. THEN dst pixels' low 24 bits = C[1..3] — a positive identification, exactly the brief's proposal. And it works BECAUSE the intervening PUTs also write the same slot — actually the first XFER GET inherits from the paint PUT directly (same slot). After that it's self-sustaining. The catch I found: the brief's proposal says "paint a first pixel with a distinctive colour so buf holds known bytes, then read a second pixel at a mismatched depth" — correct shape, and the paint must be a PUT (or a depth-4 GET) so that buf[0..3] specifically are known. For PATH A (0x1320 PUT writes 8, arms fill 4): the stale bytes are buf[4..7], which NO PUT arm ever writes. To make buf[4..7] known: a preceding GET at depth 8 (0x1320 GET) from known memory fills buf[0..7] — then a PUT at 0x1320 elsewhere writes buf[4..7] = those known bytes to dst[4..7]. Oracle: dst[4..7] == known src memory bytes. But intervening calls at the same slot between the two — in the XFER loop, GET(src) and PUT(dst) alternate at the same slot: set src_mode = 0x1320 (depth 8 GET — fills all 8 from src memory) and dst_mode = 0x1320 (depth 8 PUT — writes 4 colour + 4 stale). Then pixel N's PUT stale bytes = pixel N's GET bytes 4..7 (the immediately preceding call at the same slot!) = src memory[N*8+4 .. N*8+7]. That's not just known — it's the IMMEDIATELY preceding call, minimising the window for anything else to touch the slot. Oracle: dst[N*8+4..N*8+7] == src[N*8+4..N*8+7]... wait but dst[N*8..N*8+3] = colour encoding. Hmm, what colour does the GET produce? src_mode 0x1320 = RGBA → *color = src bytes 0..3 combined; PUT RGBA writes them back to dst[0..3]. So dst[N*8+0..3] == src[N*8+0..3] (correct copy) and dst[N*8+4..7] == src[N*8+4..7] (the LEAK — a correct depth-8 implementation would write... what? If hardware depth-8 RGBA actually transfers 8 bytes, a correct emulator would copy all 8 → dst == src entirely. The bug ALSO produces dst == src entirely! VACUOUS! Hmm. Wait no — the bug produces dst[4..7] == src[4..7] BY COINCIDENCE of the frame reuse: GET filled buf[4..7] from src, PUT leaks them to dst. A correct implementation copies them properly. Same output! So the XFER-alternation oracle for PATH A is vacuous — the leak and the correct behaviour coincide. To witness PATH A you must DECOUPLE: fill buf[4..7] with known bytes that are NOT the correct output. E.g.: paint pass = GET at 0x1320 from address P (known bytes K4..K7), then PUT at 0x1320 to dst whose own memory at [4..7] holds DIFFERENT known bytes D4..D7, with a colour known — assert dst[4..7] == K4..K7 (leak) not D4..D7 (unchanged... no — a "correct" depth-8 transfer would write SOMETHING at 4..7; what? Unknown — hardware behaviour unknown! The honest oracle is: dst[4..7] == K4..K7 proves the bytes came from the stack slot, i.e., positive identification of uninit-stack content reaching guest memory. Any fix (memset/decline/pad) makes dst[4..7] != K4..K7 (zeroes / unchanged / replicated colour). But between the paint GET and the witness PUT, the XFER loop's own structure interleaves... if the probe uses draw_primitive with XFER_EN, GET and PUT both fire per pixel. To decouple, use TWO SEPARATE primitives: first a primitive that ends with a GET at 0x1320 (e.g. XFER with src=0x1320, dst=TLB_A invalid-bit-clear so the PUT returns early WITHOUT touching buf... wait the PUT's buf is the same slot — a PUT that returns early (before buf usage, e.g. dst TLB_A with valid bit clear → returns at the tileptr check, BEFORE declaring buf) — hmm, if it returns before declaring buf, does it still use the same stack slot? The frame is the same size regardless (compiler allocates max frame for the function) — buf's slot is reserved even on early-return paths at -O3 typically... not guaranteed. Ugh. Simpler decoupling: paint with a LINE op (no XFER): draw_primitive LINE at dst_mode=0x1320... LINE's PUT writes buf[0..3] colour, leaves buf[4..7] — no. Paint with what fills buf[4..7] with known values? ONLY a GET at depth 8 does that (any colour mode; the combine doesn't matter). A GET happens in the LINE loop only when ROP != COPY (line 794: oldcolor GET). So: LINE, dst_mode=0x1320, ROP=XOR → per pixel: GET (fills buf[0..7] from dst memory — known, we pre-wrote dst memory) then PUT (writes buf[0..3]=colour, buf[4..7]=the GET's bytes → written back to the SAME dst location+... same pixel! dst[4..7] gets back the bytes just read from that same dst pixel → vacuous again (read-modify-write of the same location is invisible). Need the GET and PUT at DIFFERENT addresses. XFER does that (src ≠ dst) but then the leak equals a correct copy. LINE+ROP reads and writes the same pixel. Hmm: XFER with src=0x1320 GET then dst=0x1320 PUT: leak produces dst[4..7]=src[4..7]; "correct 8-byte copy" also produces that. Vacuous AS A DISTINGUISHER OF BUG VS CORRECT-HARDWARE — but NOT vacuous as a distinguisher of BUG VS FIXED-EMULATOR: fixed emulator (memset) → dst[4..7]=0; (decline) → unchanged; (pad) → colour replication. The probe's job is to detect the defect in THIS tree and regressions of the FIX — the oracle "dst[4..7] == src[4..7] where a memset fix yields 0" DOES discriminate bug from memset-fix... but wait, it equals src — is that "passes by luck"? No: it's a positive identification that the bytes came from the preceding GET's fill of the same stack slot — deterministic given frame reuse. The luck component is only frame reuse itself, which the same-call-site alternation makes near-certain. And the control row: same program with dst=0x1220 (matched depth 4) must NOT show src[4..7] at dst (transfer is only 4 bytes — dst[4..7] of the group untouched). Hmm, actually with depth 4, pixel stride is 4 bytes so "dst[4..7]" belongs to the next pixel — the control design needs care, but it's doable: keep dst memory pre-filled with a sentinel, use depth-4, assert no 8-byte group show