regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:777:  3. The probe's XFER_STEPX = 4 "must equal src bufdepth or the XFER arm warns" — note: with src_mode = 0x1020 (bufdepth 1), step_x must be 1, else fatal (announce-only, continues — fatal doesn't exit, so the draw still happens; but the probe wants clean output). So "ONE CONSTANT CHANGED" is slightly optimistic: for a PATH B witness via XFER, DE_XFER_STEPX must change too (4→1) to keep step_x == src_bufdepth, or accept the announce. Minor. Also the probe's setup paints SRC and DST and uses (x2,y2)=(0,3) four rows at ofs 0 — for a buf-depth witness the addressing details differ (ofs = x & 4095 with x stepping by step_x per column...). Anyway — apparatus reuse claim basically holds; "one constant" is a slight undercount (two registers: MODE and STEPX, or use the LINE/RECT PUT path for PATH A which needs no XFER).
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:801:  Q3: missing members? Add: (6) consume-side clamp — build *color from only the bytes actually read (zero-extend), transfer unchanged. Observably ≈ (2) for GET but leaves PUT... no wait, that's GET-only; for PUT the arms already fill ≥... hmm, (6) as stated fixes only PATH B. Also (7): transfer-side pairing — transfer min(bufdepth, arm width) on PUT AND read-then-zero-extend on GET — that's (5)+(6). The option set as enumerated covers the space reasonably: {pre-zero all, pre-zero len, pad fills, decline, clamp transfer}; the consume-side clamp is the one unnamed member. In-tree idiom: latched decline (#497–#500, sgirandompixel) is the dominant precedent for "guest-chosen combination we don't model"; step_x vs src_bufdepth at :734 is the one existing cross-field check and it announce-and-continues (does NOT reconcile). No in-tree precedent for reconciling two mode fields into agreement. So: idiom favours (4) latched decline of mismatched pairs; but (1) memset(sizeof) is the smallest behavioural delta that keeps every documented combination functioning and makes the guest see zeroes — with the honest "hardware unknown" comment the brief describes. The project precedent ("pick the one that is not UB and say so") supports (1) or (4). I'd flag: (4) changes behaviour for CI+16/32bpp — a combination NetBSD's actual crmfb driver... does real hardware CI mode use bufdepth... CI is 8bpp colour-index: CI+depth-1 is the MATCHED case; CI+16/32 is the mismatch. Declining mismatched CI could break a real driver IF one exists that uses it — unknown. memset (1) never breaks a combination, just zeroes. Note (1) costs 8 bytes per pixel — negligible. Also note interaction with sgirandomcolour: memset does NOT remove random() from the default arm — the brief says so correctly. And option (4) with latch would ALSO incidentally cover YCRCB? No — YCRCB is a colour-mode default, not a depth mismatch; decline-pair checks colour/depth agreement, YCRCB at MATCHED depth (0xf0 + depth 4 → default arm random 4 bytes, write 4) still random. Correct: separate.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:806:  - PATH A witness: 0x1320 PUT; carrier = a depth-8 GET (0x1320 GET fills buf[0..7] from known memory) immediately preceding; assert dst tail bytes == the GET's known source tail bytes. Caution: with XFER src and dst BOTH depth 8 and RGBA, the leak output coincides with a hypothetical correct 8-byte copy — the oracle discriminates bug-vs-FIXED (memset → 0; decline → unchanged; pad → replicated colour), not bug-vs-correct-hardware. Since hardware truth is unknown, the detector's post-fix expectation must encode the CHOSEN policy. Also note: PATH A does NOT need XFER at all — a plain RECTANGLE PUT at dst_mode 0x1320 works; and the carrier can be a preceding ROP GET at the same depth... simpler: two rectangles: rect1 GETs (ROP_XOR with dst=0x1320... the GET reads dst pixel itself — vacuous risk)... simplest decoupled carrier: XFER with src 0x1320 (GET fills 8 from src) + dst 0x1320 (PUT writes 8). The coincidence caveat above applies only to the "what would hardware do" question, not to bug-vs-fix discrimination. OK.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:807:  - Is there a simpler oracle? Alternative: assert on the ANNOUNCE (if fix = decline, the latch message IS the oracle — deterministic, no frame reliance). That's for disposition (4). For (1) memset, behavioural oracle needed. Not simpler, just different.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:854:  Q3 answer: add the consume-side clamp member (zero-extend *color from the bytes actually read) — observably ≈ (2) for GET only. In-tree precedent grep: no reconcile-into-agreement precedent; house idiom is latched decline (#497–#500, sgirandompixel — all in THIS file), plus announce-and-continue at :734 (the only existing cross-field check: step_x vs src_bufdepth). Dispositions (1) and (4) both defensible; (1) preserves all documented combos, (4) matches idiom but changes observable behaviour for CI×16/32 (which the MTE path currently EXERCISES — a decline there would fire on... does any real guest use MTE fill with DEPTH_16/32? The PROM zero-fill uses LINEAR path (depth must be 8, i.e. field 0 → 8<<0=8 ✓ that's why the check passes). TLB_A/B/C fill path with depth 16/32 — NetBSD crmfb_fill_rect uses it per the comment at 1322. If NetBSD uses 32bpp fills via MTE TLB path, disposition (4) would announce-decline on a REAL driver — that argues for (1) memset over (4) decline. Good design input: check what NetBSD crmfb does... can't easily; but the risk alone favours (1). Also (1) is what #95's ethos ("deterministic zeroes") suggests. Recommend (1) with the honest hardware-unknown comment + a comment that the transfer width and fill width intentionally differ.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:899:  Idiom grep results: there is **no in-tree precedent for reconciling two independent guest-chosen mode fields into agreement**. The precedents are: latched decline (#497–#500, sgirandompixel — all in this file), announce-and-continue (`step_x` check, :734), and clamp-to-buffer (OB-14, a length, not a mode pair). Note a force against disposition (4) that the brief didn't weigh: **the MTE path always exercises the mismatched CI combination** (next paragraph), so a decline-pair latch would announce on the `crmfb_fill_rect`-style path a real OS is documented to use (comment at :1322). That pushes to **(1) `memset(buf, 0, sizeof buf)`**: invisible in every matched case, deterministic zeroes in mismatched ones, honest "hardware behaviour unknown" comment — the disposition this project has taken before, and the one #95's own ethos suggests.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:909:  - PATH A's witness needs a depth-8 GET (`0x1320`) immediately before the depth-8 PUT with src ≠ dst, asserting dst tail == the GET's known source tail. Caveat to state in the round: when src and dst layouts align, the leak output *coincides* with a hypothetical correct 8-byte copy — so that oracle discriminates **bug-vs-fixed-emulator** (memset→0, decline→unchanged, pad→replicated), not bug-vs-hardware. With hardware truth unknown, the post-fix expectation encodes the chosen policy; say so in the detector comment.
