regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\ollama_minimax.txt:23:**D3. PREMISE 6 (fatal returns) CANNOT BE SETTLED FROM THE PACKET.** The entire analysis rests on `fatal()` returning so the `break;` after `pcc2_width_decline(...)` is reached. The inlined excerpt from `debugmsg.c` is empty -- there is no inline of `fatal()` at all. The brief asserts this as fact. **A seat that does not see `debugmsg.c` cannot ratify this.** If `fatal()` does not return, the decline arm falls through to the next case label after the missing `break;`, and the rows measure entirely the wrong behavior. The single fact that would settle it: the body of `fatal()` in `GXEMUL-SEC/src/debugmsg.c`.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\ollama_minimax.txt:27:**D5. SHAPE NOT IN THE ENUMERATION slips through at every non-SCCERR block (REASONED).** The six shapes are: guard deleted, `len > 2`, exit(1), alias, fall-through, operand swap. A seventh shape -- `len != 1` -> `len > 1 && len < 4` (decline only halfword, allow word) -- is undetected at every block except SCCERR. Reasoning: the probe exercises halfword writes at every block; under this mutant the halfword is still declined, so the readback rows still see the pre-decline byte, the decline rows still count one announce, and the survival rows still see exit0. Only SCCERR has word-write rows (`wwr sccerr`, `wwr sccerr dcl`), which is what makes M_LENGT2_SCCERR killable. The brief's claim that the enumeration is closed therefore depends on the choice of six shapes, not on the shape space.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\ollama_minimax.txt:41:**Why it slips:** the probe's wide writes are halfword (len=2), which is still declined. `rb genctl` sees 0x11, `rb genctl dcl` sees 1, `hwr genctl` sees exit0, `hwr genctl lv` sees 0x11, `latch all` sees 12 announces (the affected block still declines). Only `wwr sccerr dcl` would catch this shape, and it does so only at SCCERR.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\ollama_deepseek.txt:4:REASONED: That is not a decline of the PUT; it is only a decline of the random value. The residual `sgirandomskipput` records exactly this unwanted write. Member 3 is therefore insufficient: the correct repair is the fuller form — an explicit success result, with every GET site suppressing its dependent PUT. Option (ii) is a symptom of this: zeroing `*color` would still write, so it is not a valid alternative.  
