regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:435:   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: a DECLINED pixel GET still lets the caller perform its dependent PUT, so the destination is written with fg",
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:437:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06 from the `sgirandompixel' design panel, where **codex argued for it as part of that round\nand grok argued for filing it -- and grok's scoping was taken**, because a return value plus every GET site\nis not ONE SITE and the reopening rule says a round takes one.\n\nAfter `sgirandompixel', `horrible_getputpixel()' leaves `*color' alone on a declined GET, so the caller's\nown `uint32_t color = fg' stands and the PUT writes the FOREGROUND.  That is DETERMINISTIC, which is the\nwhole gain of that round -- and it is still a write of data the guest did not ask for.\n\n**codex's form, recorded verbatim because it is the design if this is taken:** give the function an explicit\nSUCCESS RESULT; *'unchanged `*color' is merely an output-parameter guarantee: callers must not consume it\nfollowing failure'*.  Cover ALL the GET sites -- the source GET, the `oldcolor' GET used by ROPs, and MTE's\n`bg' GET.  **AND ITS WARNING, which is the sketch rule and is the reason this is not a one-liner:**\n*'preserve coordinate advancement and stipple rotation when suppressing a pixel's PUT; an early `continue'\ncan bypass those updates'* -- anchors `src_x += step_x' and `Rotate the stipple pattern'.\n\nThe shipped detector already discriminates this: `lin_b is fg' expects fg TODAY, and under a skip-PUT fix it\nwould expect the PRE-PAINT instead.  So the oracle-correction rule applies again and the row set is known in\nadvance.",
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:447:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06.  `horrible_getputpixel()' implements tlb_mode 0, 1, 2 and 4; **LIN_B extracts to 5 and\nfalls to the default arm**, which now declines.  `re_linear_b[]' exists, is written by the same width-checked\nstore path as `re_linear_a[]', and case 4 is four lines -- so the tempting fix is one more case.\n\n*** THE PANEL REFUSED TO ENDORSE THAT, and the reasoning is the ADDITION LICENCE working. ***  codex: case 4\nitself carries `TODO... probably not correct!'; and the MTE path that does select either array checks\n`entry & 0x80000000' for validity and searches BY PAGE, while case 4 indexes DIRECTLY and FORCES validity.\n**Symmetry between the two arrays is supporting evidence, not a transferred warrant for DE translation.**\nBefore implementing, obtain a DE address/validity reference or a hardware comparison using DISTINCT A/B\nmappings.\n\nIt also caught that the witness probe programs only LINEAR_A, so *'merely changing its expected LIN_B result\nto SRC_VAL would be unjustified'* -- the instrument would be asserting a behaviour nothing established.",
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:457:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06.  **FOUND BY GROK** on the `sgirandompixel' design panel, as a residual it explicitly said\nto file rather than grow the round with: *'`default:' of the COLOUR-mode switch still does\n`buf[n] = random()' and then writes those bytes.  Same class, different arm.'*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING before filing**: four `random()' calls at dev_sgi_re.c, anchor `buf[0] = random();',\nin the colour-mode `default:'.  `sgirandompixel' removed the ONE in `horrible_getputpixel()'; these four\nremain and are the reason `grep -c 'random()'` on that file is still non-zero after the fix.\n\nNobody else on that panel saw it -- not codex, not the packet-fed seats, not me -- and I had read the file\nseveral times that evening.  The design is presumably the same member 3 (decline + latched announce), but\nthe observable differs: this arm fills a BUFFER rather than a single caller variable, so what a declined\nfill should leave is its own question and the detector shape is not simply copied.",
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:465:   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: the tileptr high-bit arm declines SILENTLY -- the sibling of sgirandompixel's arm, with no announce at all",
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:467:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06 from `sgirandompixel''s pre-table SIBLING PARITY check, which is what the check is for.\n\nThe nearest sibling of the mode arm is the `if (!(tileptr & 0x80000000))' check immediately below it: also a\ndisabled kill (`//exit(1)'), also in `horrible_getputpixel()', also reached on a failed GET.  It returns\nWITHOUT touching `*color' -- which is exactly the behaviour `sgirandompixel' gave the mode arm, **so after\nthat round the two siblings AGREE on the value and DIFFER on the announce**: the mode arm now says so once,\nand this one says nothing at all.\n\nWhether that asymmetry should be closed is a real question rather than an obvious yes: a silent decline is\noption-set member 2, and the pass-2 seat on that panel noted the tileptr arm *'safely suppresses a put, but a\nget leaves foreground -- or the previous copied pixel -- in place'*, which is a DIFFERENT observable from the\nmode arm's.  Sibling parity says name the asymmetry, not that every asymmetry is a defect.",
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:486:  One more check on the vacuity repair: with the 1x4 rectangle, do four rows really rewrite the same byte? LIN_A: ofs = x & 4095, x=0 always (x1=0, x2=0 → only x=0), y goes 0..3 but ignored. tile_nr = x >> 12 = 0. tileptr = 0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[0] << 12) = phys 0x10000. ofs = 0. So all four pixels PUT to phys 0x10000 with bufdepth 4 (BUFDEPTH_32 → (mode>>8)&3 = 2 → bufdepth 4). So each PUT writes 4 bytes at the same address; the value written each time is `color` = fg (post-fix) — same value each time → final value fg. Oracle survives. Confirmed by BASE.out row 7: 0x77665544. And the diag count: 4 GET declines → unlatched would print 4, latched prints 1. Confirmed by M_NOLATCH (4) vs BASE (1).
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:492:  Now the design panel question (i): member 3 (DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE) in grok's MINIMAL scope vs codex's fuller form. Let me read the design_panel/brief.md and grok.txt/codex.txt to verify the panel actually chose this and what members 2/3 were. This matters for answering (i) honestly.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:494:  Also for (iv): `lin_b not src` — non-discriminating, true before and after. Kept as a regression guard against IMPLEMENT-as-copy. That's its only discriminating power: if someone implements LIN_B correctly (sgirelinb), got_b == SRC_VAL → negate row FAILS. So it's not vacuous — it discriminates exactly one future change (the filed sgirelinb being taken silently without re-pinning the probe). It also cannot be reddened by any mutant of THIS fix's shape (decline family), which is why the packet calls it non-discriminating for this table. Keeping it costs nothing and guards the oracle from going stale relative to the ledger row. Defensible.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:496:  Let me read the design panel files.Now I understand the option set: DELETE(1) · DECLINE silent(2) · DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE(3) · ACCEPT(4) · FAULT(5) · BACKED ACCEPT(6) · IMPLEMENT(7). Member 3 = decline + latched announce. The packet says the panel chose member 3 in grok's MINIMAL scope, with codex's fuller form (success result + suppress PUT at every GET site) filed as sgirandomskipput.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:498:  Let me quickly check grok.txt and codex.txt to see whether the panel story holds.I'll check the four premises against `GXEMUL-SEC/src/` first, then decide the option-set member and what happens to `*color` from the arm and its callers.Premises look plausible so far; next I’ll read the function, callers, and how LIN_A vs LIN_B are actually stored.MTE already treats LIN_B as the other linear array; I’ll read that path, the DE mode stores, latches, and the witness probe next.Checking whether a real driver programs `DE_MODE_LIN_B`, and how `FAULT` is dispatched from this call path.**MEMBER 3: DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE; `*color` UNTOUCHED.** All four numbered premises hold.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:537:`lin_b is fg` is the inverting oracle. It is also the discriminator against the rejected dispositions: zero leaves `0`, skip-PUT leaves `0x11111111`, IMPLEMENT-as-copy leaves `0xaabbccdd`. `lin_b diag once` is the discriminator against silent decline (member 2). Drop `lin_b written` as a defect row; under this member it is true before and after.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:615:  Kill-table denominator: 8 rows × 5 mutants + opening/closing BASE. All five mutant shapes swept: verbatim reinstatement (b), nulled repair (d), latch dropped, silent decline (member 2), exit restored. I re-derived every score by diffing .out files against BASE.out myself: M_RANDOM 6/8 (fg row + latch row), M_ZERO 7/8 (fg row, 0x0), M_NOLATCH 7/8 (latch row 4), M_SILENT 6/8 (both diag rows), M_EXIT 4/8 (4 rows, died). BASE_final identical to BASE. driver.log confirms clean clone, no sentinel, frozen probe md5 1daecf60 matches the shipped probe.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:621:  One more possible check: "(once)" in message text — house idiom. The new message ends with "declined (once) ]" — hmm, looking at the format string: "horrible_getputpixel (%s), x=%i y=%i; declined (once) ]\n" — wait let me look: "fatal("[ sgi_re: unimplemented dst_mode %i for horrible_getputpixel (%s), x=%i y=%i; declined (once) ]\n", ...)" — yes "(once)" present. Good.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:668:  - The diff vs HEAD `79cd12e` is exactly as described: pre-fix was `fatal(...); // exit(1); *color = random(); return;` (confirmed via `git show HEAD:`); post-fix declines, `*color` untouched, latch `SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10` as the last enumerator with a correct "next free 1 << 11" note; caller's `uint32_t color = fg` stands (`:843`).
