regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:88:     c) **The `d->warned` field width**: the product diff adds a typedef bounding PCC2_W_NBITS <= 32. What if someone changes the latch to announce but not break? E.g., remove the `break;` after pcc2_width_decline in one arm — that's the fall-through shape, caught at the four arms with new rows and at arms with readbacks. Wait — actually, look at the fall-through shape carefully: "decline nulled, guard kept" vs "guard kept, its effect removed". The M_FALL mutants presumably keep the announce AND land the write (remove the `break`). At arms with readbacks (GENCTL, TxCTL, TxICR, SCCTX, MASK, and now SCCICR/SCCRX/SCSIICR via the new rows), the readback catches it. At PSCALEADJ, the "ignored" print catches it. At VECBASE — rb vecbase catches it? rb vecbase: the row checks VECBASE unchanged after a declined halfword to GENCTL... wait, "rb vecbase" — "VECBASE unchanged too -- the second lane of the declined halfword". Hmm, that's about a GENCTL write not touching VECBASE. Is there a row where a wide write TO VECBASE is declined and read back? VECBASE is odd, operator-reachable. The op vecbase row: `put h` at VECBASE survives + dcl. No readback at VECBASE itself! So a fall-through at VECBASE: announce happens (op vecbase dcl stays 1), and the halfword lands... what row sees it? rb vecbase checks VECBASE after a write to GENCTL — different cell. Hmm, but wait: does VECBASE's arm even have a store? VECBASE's write body — unknown from the inlined code. The fall-through at VECBASE would store idata's lane into VECBASE. Is any row reading VECBASE after a wide write to VECBASE? Looking at the rows: "rb vecbase" reads VECBASE after st.h to GENCTL (r5=0x50 set by byte first? Actually the session: setup(r5=0x50, r6=0xaabb) + program ST_B(5, VECBASE)? Not sure — the row is from the 60-row part, not fully inlined). PRETABLE Section 2 lists VECBASE's readback as "rb vecbase". If rb vecbase does: set VECBASE=0x50 by byte, put h 0xaabb at GENCTL (even, aligned 0x02), read VECBASE back expecting 0x50 — that checks the GENCTL write didn't bleed into VECBASE's lane. A fall-through AT VECBASE (put h at 0x03) would store... at odd address 0x03, a halfword spans 0x03-0x04 — spans VECBASE and the next register. Hmm, actually what does the device do with relative_addr 0x03 len 2? The guard is per-block at relative_addr. A fall-through at VECBASE stores (uint8_t)idata = 0xbb at... relative_addr 0x03 = VECBASE itself. So VECBASE becomes 0xbb. Would any row see that? Only if a row reads VECBASE after a wide write TO VECBASE. I don't see one in the diff. So VECBASE × fall-through may be UNMEASURED and NOT covered by a channel. Hmm, but wait — PRETABLE Section 2 says VECBASE's readback is "rb vecbase". If rb vecbase's session only writes wide to GENCTL, it does not exercise a VECBASE fall-through. Let me check the actual probe file to see rb vecbase's recipe. The diff only shows the 60->82 additions; rb vecbase is in the old 60. The inlined diff shows at line ~325: `t, alive, how = session(setup(r5="0x50", r6="0xaabb")` ... `row("rb vecbase", printed(t, "r4") if alive else "died", "0x50", "VECBASE unchanged too -- the second lane of the declined halfword")`. The context line before shows it's part of a session — but the program isn't shown (it's context from the old file; the diff hunk starts mid-session). "the second lane of the declined halfword" — so the session writes a halfword at GENCTL (0x02) and checks both GENCTL (rb genctl) and VECBASE (rb vecbase) unchanged. It does NOT write wide to VECBASE itself. So a M_FALL at VECBASE (announce + land) is caught by... nothing? op vecbase dcl stays 1 (announce kept), op vecbase exit0, rb vecbase unchanged (no wide write to VECBASE in its session). So VECBASE × fall-through is a LIVE UNCOVERED CELL — unless VECBASE's body stores nothing (like PSCALEADJ). What's VECBASE's write body? Not inlined. In real gxemul dev_pcc2.c, PCCTWO_VECBASE write: `fatal("[ pcc2: write to PCCTWO_VECBASE...")` — actually in the pristine source, VECBASE write had a fatal. In the repaired fork, the body presumably stores idata? Unknown. I should check the actual file. This is checkable — GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:94:     What about IPL × fall-through? IPL's write body: prints "write to read-only PCCTWO_IPL; ignored" once (static int w latch) and stores nothing. A fall-through at IPL: width decline announces (hwr ipl dcl=1), then falls into `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) { static int w... fatal("write to read-only PCCTWO_IPL; ignored") }`. The message "write to read-only PCCTWO_IPL; ignored" would print — is there a row counting it at 0? No! There's no "hwr ipl noig" row analogous to "op pscale noig". So IPL × fall-through is UNMEASURED: the fall-through's only effect is a second message, which no row counts. Blast: none (store nothing), but the guard's effect (skip the body) is removed with no channel. By the brief's own floor logic ("the guard kept, its effect removed" is a floor breach), IPL × fall-through is a breach that escapes. However — is it? The effect of the guard at IPL is to prevent the "ignored" print (and the read-only refusal). The floor question: does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the clause-(d) enumeration? A fall-through at IPL: all 82 rows stay green? hwr ipl dcl counts "write to PCCTWO_IPL declined" = 1 ✓ (announce kept). No row counts "read-only" prints. So yes — M_FALL_IPL sweeps 82/82. Wait, unless the "read-only" fatal... fatal returns, prints once. So the session continues. hwr ipl lv expects r4 = 0x5a5a5a5a — unaffected. So M_FALL_IPL is a clean-sweep mutant at an even, guest-reachable arm. That is exactly the floor question the brief asks to be closed, and the four new rows cover only SCCICR/SCCRX/SCSIICR/PSCALEADJ. IPL was given rows for kill-reinstatement but NOT for fall-through.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:96:     Hold on — but is a fall-through at IPL meaningful? The body's effect is only a message; the decline's effect is to suppress that message. The floor clause (d): "the guard kept, its effect removed". The guard's effect at IPL is to skip the read-only-refusal print. Removing the break lets the print happen. Is that a "breach" that matters? By the brief's own standard — PSCALEADJ's fall-through was ruled a floor breach precisely because the only channel was a print ("op pscale noig" counts the "ignored" print at 0). IPL is in the SAME class: body stores nothing, only prints. PSCALEADJ got a noig row; IPL did not. That's an internal inconsistency in the class closure: the class "announce-then-land at an arm whose body has no store" includes BOTH PSCALEADJ and IPL, and only PSCALEADJ got the row. Actually wait — at IPL the landed write still doesn't store (IPL body never stores), so there's no state corruption, only a spurious print. At PSCALEADJ likewise (no store, only print). The brief ruled PSCALEADJ's fall-through a floor breach needing a row. By parity, IPL's is too. The escape is real within the brief's own rule system.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:98:     Hmm wait, but actually let me re-check: would M_FALL_IPL really pass all 82 rows? The fall-through is: guard kept (announce via pcc2_width_decline), `break` removed. Then `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) { static int w = 0; if (!w) { fatal(...); w = 1; } }`. fatal prints "[ pcc2: write to read-only PCCTWO_IPL; ignored ]". Row check: "hwr ipl dcl" counts "write to PCCTWO_IPL " + DECL = 1 ✓. Gate rows don't include an IPL-ignored count. So yes, green. Also `latch all`: 12 announces ✓ (announce kept). So M_FALL_IPL = 82/82 sweep. That's a concrete answer to Question 2 (smallest edit that breaks this and still passes): delete the `break;` after the decline in the IPL arm — a one-token edit, in the ALREADY-ENUMERATED fall-through shape, at a block the repair classified but did not give the fall-through row. It keeps the announce (unlike a delete), so every dcl row stays green; it stores nothing, so every readback stays green; its only observable is the "read-only ... ignored" print, which no row counts at 0.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:100:     Hmm, wait — but actually, would the "ignored" print even be wrong? On the fix, a wide write to IPL: width-guard declines (correct per direction split: writes DECLINE). With the fall-through, it ALSO prints "write to read-only IPL; ignored" — which is arguably the correct legacy behavior for a write (IPL is read-only)! So is the fall-through at IPL even a defect? By the repair's own contract, the width guard should decline and skip the body. The body printing "ignored" is the len==1 path behavior. A wide write producing BOTH messages is off-contract but benign. The brief's floor logic, though, doesn't grade by benign-ness: "a guard kept with its decline nulled while the write lands (announce-then-land) at the four odd arms was 'a sentence owed'... the builder asked whether a disclosure can discharge a floor clause. It cannot." The fall-through at IPL is the same shape. If PSCALEADJ's print-only fall-through merits a row, IPL's does too. Either the class is {arms where fall-through has a channel} and IPL is in it, or the rule is "state-changing fall-throughs need rows" — but PSCALEADJ is not state-changing and got a row. So inconsistent.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:106:     - Question (b): can benign traffic set a bit early? The `latch all` session is a fresh emulator session (each `session()` spawns a new instance presumably). put h at each offset — each put h is one wide write per block. Within the session, nothing else touches pcc2. But WAIT: the debugger `put h` at odd addresses "warns, then stores" — the warn is a debugger-side warning, not a pcc2 message, so DECL count unaffected. ✓. But what about `put h` at EVEN addresses: 0x02, 0x16, 0x1a, 0x1c, 0x1e, 0x3e — a halfword write at 0x02 lands at GENCTL arm with len=2 → decline. ✓ one announce each. At ODD addresses 0x03 etc: relative_addr=0x03 → VECBASE arm. ✓. But hold on — `put h 0xfff42003`: does the debugger's store_16bit_word do ONE memory_rw(len 2) at addr 0x03, or two byte stores? Premise 4 says ONE memory_rw(len 2). OK.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:107:     - Two blocks announcing on one bit while count stays 12: if SCCTX and SCCRX shared a bit, first put h at SCCTX announces (count 1 for that pair), put h at SCCRX: bit already set → silent. Count drops to 11. ✓ caught. The question asks: can two blocks announce on one bit and STILL count 12? Only if some block announces TWICE — e.g., if one offset maps to two arms? A put h at 0x16 len 2 hits relative_addr 0x16 only. One arm per access. Could the put h at some offset trigger announces at two blocks? No — one DEVICE_ACCESS call, one arm, one decline. Could a block announce twice in this session? Each offset is written once; d->warned latches per bit. So 12 distinct bits → 12 announces; any collision → ≤11. Sound — PROVIDED each of the 12 offsets actually reaches a distinct arm. What about T1CTL 0x17? Not in the list — TxCTL appears once (0x16), so the shared bit is exercised once. Fine: 12 bits, 12 offsets. ✓.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:108:     - One more: the value 0x0003 "fits a byte, so this row ALSO reds if any arm swaps its guard operand from len to a value test" — wait, does it? If an arm swaps `len != 1` to `idata > 0xff`, then a put h 0x0003 (idata=3 ≤ 0xff) is NOT declined → no announce → count drops to 11 → latch all reds. ✓. And the write lands: 0x0003 stored... at GENCTL the byte store sets GENCTL = 0x03. Does any check catch that beyond the count? latch all lv (host survives) — storing 0x03 at GENCTL: bit 0x04 clear (0x03 = bits 0,1), so no c040kill. OK. And the count is the channel. ✓.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:109:     - BUT: subtle — under the operand swap at an ODD arm, e.g. MASK: put h 0x0003 at 0x3f... the swap makes guard pass (no decline), body stores idata&0xff=0x03 at MASK and calls reassert_interrupts. Harmless per the MASK comment (GENCTL's C040 bit clear). Count 11. ✓ red.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:112:     Now (c): is `op pscale noig` a real channel? The row: session(["put h 0x%x, 0x0303" % (PCC2 + PSCALEADJ)]) then counts "PSCALEADJ ignored" expecting 0. On the fix: put h at 0x15 (odd) → warn, one memory_rw len 2 → PSCALEADJ arm, len!=1 & write → decline, break. "ignored" print skipped. Count 0 ✓. Under M_FALL_PSCALEADJ: decline announces, then falls to `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) fatal("[ pcc2: write to PSCALEADJ ignored (value 0x%02x) ]\n")` → prints once → count 1 ✓ red. Does fatal's message reach the pty transcript? The put h is a debugger command; the session transcript t — the probe counts strings in the transcript. fatal prints to stderr/console... In gxemul, debugmsg/fatal print to stderr (and console?). The other rows count DECL strings ("write to PCCTWO_X -- declined. (once)") from the same fatal() channel and those are measured working (op pscale dcl counts DECL=1, measured in part 1 table? M_FALL_PSCALEADJ row red on "op pscale noig" — measured 81/82 with exactly that row red ✓ MEASURED). So the channel is measured real. ✓. And is 0 the right expectation on the fix? The session does only the put h; no byte write to PSCALEADJ. ✓ 0.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:116:     (d): the three new readbacks on the FIX: set byte 0x1f (st.b legal), put h 0xaab8 (declined), ld.bu → expect 0x1f. On the fix, no reassert issue: the byte store of 0x1f DOES call reassert_interrupts (body executes for len==1!). Wait — st.b 0x1f to SCCICR: len==1 → guard skipped → body: store 0x1f & 0x1f, idata & ~0x1f = 0 → no HUH, reassert_interrupts(d). So on the FIX, the byte store reasserts. Does that end the host? The pcc2c040kill class: reassert with GENCTL bit 0x04... Premise 7: every GENCTL lane value in the probe keeps bit 0x04 clear. The byte store 0x1f to SCCICR sets interrupt-control bits; reassert could raise an interrupt to the CPU — fine, host survives (measured: BASE3 82/82, so green on fix). Under fall-through: put h 0xaab8 lands 0x18 (&0x1f) or 0x98 (&0xdf), reasserts — could THAT end the host? If it did, the row reads "died" — also red, as the brief says ("Either is red, but the record should say which"). Measured: M_FALL_SCCICR 81/82, red row = "rb sccicr" — and the value? The log shows the row red; whether it read 0x18 or died isn't stated in the inlined table (just "rb sccicr" listed as red). The brief asks "the record should say which" — the inlined table doesn't distinguish died vs wrong-value. Minor record gap: the pass-3c table lists the red row names but not the observed values. Actually for an adversarial review: the question (d) is answered by measurement (3c: each M_FALL reds exactly its row), but the brief itself asks whether reassert could END THE HOST — and the measurement shows the row red but doesn't say died vs 0x18/0x98. Since the row was red (not the session's other rows), and if the host had died, would other rows be affected? Each session is independent, so a death inside the rb sccicr session would mark that row "died" only. The table can't distinguish. So the brief's own standard ("the record should say which") is not met by the inlined record. Nit.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:157:     Note also: hrd ipl expects exit0 and hrd ipl lv expects 0x5a5a5a5a. LD_H at IPL on the fix: len=2 read → guard requires MEM_WRITE → skipped → read served by... the memcpy above? The IPL arm read side: `if (d->pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_IPL] == 0) { fatal("IPL == 0 on read") }` — else nothing. So read of len 2 at 0x3e: memory_readmax64 reads 2 bytes (0x3e, 0x3f = IPL, MASK). On the fix, is a len-2 READ at IPL declined? The guard only declines writes. Reads ACCEPT (direction split). So hrd ipl reads IPL||MASK composed big-endian. r4 gets... 0x5a5a5a5a is the scratch/liveness pattern (I_ST_SCR/I_LD_SCR — store scratch sentinel around, verify alive). printed r4 = 0x5a5a5a5a means the program continued to I_LD_SCR and printed the sentinel. ✓.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:162:     1. dev_pcc2.c in GXEMUL-SEC/src: 12 guards, identical guard text, bit names distinct, VECBASE/SCCERR/MASK arm bodies, and whether `warned` is 12-bit field... Premise 6 says d->warned is "a 12-bit field". If `warned` is declared as e.g. `uint32_t warned : 12` or just an int? "a 12-bit field written ONLY by pcc2_width_decline()". The typedef bounds NBITS ≤ 32 against "the 32-bit field" — so warned is 32-bit, and "12-bit" means 12 bits USED. Wording in premise 6: "a 12-bit field" — slightly loose vs the product comment "the 32-bit field". Let me check the struct.
