regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:174:     - **The escape hatch: `writeflag == MEM_WRITE` clause.** A mutant changing the guard to `if (len != 1)` (drop writeflag) would decline READS too → hrd rows red ✓ (hrd genctl etc. exist for even blocks; odd blocks' reads via debugger? "op" rows are writes; are there odd-block READ rows? hrd only at even. A guard dropping writeflag at, say, SCCRX: reads of len 2 at SCCRX declined → what row reads SCCRX at len 2? rb sccrx uses LD_BU (len 1). Hmm — ld.bu is a byte read, len 1, guard len!=1 not tripped. So a `len != 1` (read+write) mutant at SCCRX: declines halfword reads AND writes. Wide reads at SCCRX: does any row do one? No — the new readbacks use byte reads. Would the decline of a wide READ be caught? The decline announces "unimplemented len=2 write to..." wait no — the decline message says "write to %s" but if the guard drops the writeflag check, a halfword READ would also trigger pcc2_width_decline... but would any row observe it? Only if a row does a wide read at that block. Even blocks: hrd rows do LD_H at genctl/t2ctl/t2icr/sccerr/scctx — wait no, hrd rows exist for genctl, t2ctl, t2icr, sccerr, scctx (rows 5,9,13,17,21) — hmm those are the guest-reachable even blocks. So a dropped-writeflag mutant at any EVEN block reds the hrd row (declined read... wait, does a declined READ change the read value? The guard for reads: if tripped, pcc2_width_decline announces and BREAKS — skipping the read? The read path: DEVICE_ACCESS with readflag — the data was already composed by the memcpy/readmax64 BEFORE the switch (typical gxemul pattern: on read, memory_rw reads into idata first, then the switch handles side effects; the value is returned regardless). Actually in gxemul DEVICE_ACCESS, for reads the value is read before the switch via the register array; breaking early just skips side effects. The hrd lv rows (r4 = 0x5a5a5a5a sentinel) only check survival, not the read value! hrd genctl lv expects 0x5a5a5a5a — that's the liveness sentinel, not the register value. So a dropped-writeflag mutant at an even block: hrd X survives (fatal returns), announce fires (extra DECL count — does any row count DECL=0 for reads? No)... would ANY row red? hrd genctl: exit0 ✓ still. The decline prints "unimplemented len=2 write to GENCTL -- declined" during a READ — counted by rb genctl dcl? rb genctl dcl counts "write to PCCTWO_GENCTL " + DECL in the rb genctl session — which does byte writes and one st.h. A dropped-writeflag mutant doesn't change that session's counts (the st.h still announces once; byte ops don't trip; are there wide READS in the rb genctl session? The readback is ld.bu — len 1, not tripped). Hmm, so who catches `len != 1` (writeflag dropped)? The hrd sessions: they do LD_H → guard trips → decline announces + break. Read side effect skipped: for GENCTL read, is there a side effect? Probably not. Value returned is from the pre-switch read — so LD_H still gets the value, hrd genctl lv = sentinel ✓ (sentinel, not register). exit0 ✓. hrd genctl doesn't count messages. So NO row reds?! Wait — "latch all"? No reads there. Hmm — but actually wait: does the m88k guest even DO halfword reads in hrd rows: "hrd genctl" = LD_H(4, GENCTL)? From gate list "hrd genctl" + "hrd genctl lv". If the mutant declines the read (announce+break), the session survives, prints sentinel — both rows green. The extra DECL announce goes uncounted (no row counts DECL during hrd sessions). SO: guard changed from `len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` to `len != 1` at GENCTL = 82/82 sweep?? 
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:176:     Hold on — is that in the enumerated shapes? The six shapes are: guard deleted, len>2, exit(1), latch alias, fall-through, operand swap. "writeflag clause dropped" is NOT among the six! It's a seventh shape: the guard FIRES on reads (over-coverage, not under-coverage). The floor clause (d) is about mutants that REMOVE the guard's effect. Dropping writeflag ADDS declines (reads now declined). The contract: "reads ACCEPT, writes DECLINE". A read-decline violates "reads ACCEPT" — the direction split! What row pins "reads ACCEPT"? hrd rows check survival + liveness — but do they check the read VALUE or absence of a decline message? From the gate list: "hrd genctl", "hrd genctl lv" — survival + sentinel. If a wide read gets declined, the read... let me think about what "declined read" even means in this code: the switch arm's read path for most of these registers does nothing (the value was pre-loaded). pcc2_width_decline + break → skips nothing meaningful for read → the read returns the register value anyway. And it PRINTS a decline message falsely. No row counts that. So a `writeflag`-dropping mutant is a clean sweep — and it VIOLATES the shipped contract ("reads ACCEPT") rather than just being benign. Is that a floor breach? Clause (d) floor: "the guard kept, its effect removed" — this mutant keeps and EXTENDS the guard's effect. It breaks the ACCEPT half of the direction split. The detector's contract says it catches "reinstatement of the kill" — and this mutant doesn't kill. So strictly the floor might not cover over-eager decline. But the probe contract comment says the fix IS a direction split: reads accept. A mutant that makes reads decline is a defect against the documented contract and no row sees it. Smallest edit: delete ` && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` from one arm's guard — 26 characters-ish. Rows that stay green: all 82. Actually wait — would hrd X lv still print sentinel? Yes. Unless the decline's break skips the read-return... In gxemul, DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2): on MEM_READ, idata is typically composed at the END from pcctwo_reg[relative_addr] after the switch, or BEFORE? Pattern in gxemul devices: `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) idata = d->reg[...]` happens either in each arm or a common tail. If the read value composition happens INSIDE the arm after the guard... then break skips composition → idata stays 0 (or uninitialized) → LD_H returns 0 → but hrd lv checks sentinel 0x5a5a5a5a in r4 — wait, hrd genctl lv: "print r4" after LD_H(4, GENCTL)? If r4 gets the LD_H result, then lv would be the register value not sentinel... but the fix expectation is 0x5a5a5a5a which looks like a SENTINEL (setup probably writes 0x5a5a5a5a somewhere, I_ST_SCR/I_LD_SCR store/restore scratch). Actually "hrd ipl lv" expects 0x5a5a5a5a — and the program is [I_ST_SCR, LD_H(4, IPL), I_LD_SCR] + print r4. I_LD_SCR probably loads the sentinel into r4 AFTER the LD_H (r4 clobbered then overwritten). So lv = liveness sentinel: proves execution continued past the access. It does NOT verify the read value. So a declined read is invisible to the lv rows. ✓ my analysis holds: dropping writeflag is a clean sweep UNLESS the read value is checked somewhere or the extra announce is counted.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:178:     Hmm wait, but for IPL specifically: hwr ipl dcl counts declines in the hwr ipl session. That session: [I_ST_SCR, ST_H(6, IPL), I_LD_SCR] — one wide WRITE → one decline ✓. No wide read in that session → dropping writeflag doesn't add counts there. But hrd ipl session: [I_ST_SCR, LD_H(4, IPL), I_LD_SCR] — one wide READ. Under the writeflag-dropped mutant: decline announces once (IPL bit), message "write to PCCTWO_IPL declined" printed during a READ. hrd ipl = exit0 ✓; hrd ipl lv = sentinel ✓. No dcl-count row in the hrd session. GREEN. So M_NOWRITEFLAG_IPL sweeps.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:182:     (i) Delete `&& writeflag == MEM_WRITE` from ONE arm's guard (say SCCRX, an odd arm with no wide-read row): reads at len 2 get declined — violates "reads ACCEPT" — no row. Even smaller at an odd arm: NO row does a wide read at ANY odd block (op rows are puts=writes; readbacks are ld.bu byte reads). So at an odd arm, even a hrd-style check doesn't exist. At an even arm, hrd exists but only checks survival. So the mutant sweeps at any arm. Smallest: at SCCRX, delete ` && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` (26 chars). Effect: any guest halfword READ at SCCRX... wait, SCCRX is odd — guests can't do wide reads there (alignment). Operator can via debugger `get h`? or the m88k guest at even arms only. For VECBASE (0x03): odd too. All odd arms: wide reads only via operator path. Hmm — do odd arms even have a guest-observable read path at len 2? No (alignment). So dropping writeflag at an odd arm affects only operator reads — invisible to the probe entirely, and arguably harmless. At an EVEN arm (GENCTL): guest `ld.h` at 0x02 → declined → announce lies ("write to") and... read still served? If yes, guest-visible wrongness = spurious console message only. If the break skips read composition, guest reads 0/garbage — REAL defect — and hrd genctl lv still green because it only checks the sentinel! Let me verify from the file whether read composition happens before or inside arms. In the IPL arm read side: `if (d->pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_IPL] == 0) {...}` — it READS the register array; the returned idata for reads must be composed elsewhere (common code before the switch, since the IPL read arm doesn't set idata). In gxemul dev_pcc2.c, I believe there's `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) idata = ...` near the top or the regs are memcpy'd. The premise 4/comment says "a READ the memcpy above had already served" (IPL comment: "said 'ignored' about a READ the memcpy above had already served") — so YES: reads are served by a memcpy ABOVE the switch. So a declined read still returns the right value; the only effect is a lying "declined" message on the console. So the writeflag-drop mutant's blast = spurious message per block. Contract violation ("reads ACCEPT") with no row. Is that a floor breach? The floor is about the DECLINE side. The contract comment in the probe says the fix is a direction split: reads accept. A mutant breaking the accept half, caught by nothing — that's a genuine detector gap, arguably above/beside clause (d) rather than below the floor. The brief's Question 1(a) asks if the clause-(d) enumeration is closed with SIX shapes — the six shapes all attack the DECLINE direction (make writes land). NONE attacks the ACCEPT direction (make reads decline) or the announce-integrity (e.g., wrong block name in the message — `pcc2_width_decline(d, PCC2_W_X_LEN, "PCCTWO_WRONGNAME", len)`? The name string is just printed; rows match "write to PCCTWO_X " + DECL — a swapped NAME string would change counts at arms... e.g., swap the name strings of SCCRX and SCSIICR: op sccrx dcl counts "write to PCCTWO_SCCRX declined" — under the swap, a put h at SCCRX prints "SCSIICR" → op sccrx dcl 0, op scsiicr gets 2? Each in separate sessions: op sccrx session counts SCCRX-declines = 0 (red ✓). So name swaps are caught by dcl rows ✓.)
