regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:192:     (vi) **`len != 1` → `len == 2`** at one arm: declines ONLY len-2, lands len-4 (word) writes. Which rows do len-4 writes? wwr sccerr (word write SCCERR) + wwr sccerr dcl + val w sccerr. At SCCERR: `len == 2` mutant → word write (len 4) NOT declined → lands (value-identical, no visible change) AND wwr sccerr dcl counts 0 → red ✓. At other arms: no len-4 rows exist except SCCERR! wwr only at SCCERR. So `len != 1` → `len == 2` at, say, GENCTL: len-4 writes land silently — does any row do a word access at GENCTL? No wwr genctl row. hwr (len 2) still declined → all hwr/rb rows green. Guest CAN do word writes at even offsets (m88k st.d? or st word len 4 — alignment ok at 0x02? len-4 at 0x02: 0x02 % 4 = 2 — MISALIGNED for a word access! m88k alignment check: word accesses must be 4-aligned. 0x02 is not. So a len-4 access at GENCTL (0x02) is alignment-faulted before memory_rw → guest-unreachable; operator put w at 0x02 — does the debugger warn-and-store for word at non-4-aligned? The operator path measured was put h at odd addr. put w at 0x02: misaligned for word → debugger warns, stores → memory_rw len 4 at 0x02 → GENCTL arm → under `len == 2` mutant: not declined → lands. No row does put w at GENCTL. So `len != 1` → `len == 2` at GENCTL sweeps 82/82! Is that shape in the six? Shape 2 is `len != 1` → `len > 2` (declines MORE). `len == 2` declines LESS (only len 2) — the complement: it lets len≥3 through. Hmm, `len > 2` lets len-2 through; `len == 2` lets len-4 through. Both are "guard weakened" but the measured len>2 mutants red via the halfword-landing rows (rb t2icr etc. use st.h len 2 — under len>2, halfwords LAND → readback reds). Under `len == 2`, halfwords are STILL declined → readbacks stay green; only word writes land — and only SCCERR has word rows. So `len == 2` at any arm except SCCERR sweeps. Even at GENCTL which has the most rows. Wait — "val h genctl" row: value h genctl? "val h genctl" and "val w sccerr" — val w sccerr is a word-access value row at SCCERR only. So yes: at 11 of 12 blocks, no len-4 row exists. The len==2 mutant at GENCTL: st.h declined ✓ rb green; but a len-4 guest write at GENCTL is alignment-blocked (0x02 not 4-aligned)... so what's reachable? len-4 at even blocks: which even offsets are 4-aligned? relative_addr 0x02 no, 0x16 no, 0x1a no, 0x1c: 0x1c % 4 = 0 ✓ SCCERR is 4-aligned! 0x1e no, 0x3e no. So among even blocks only SCCERR can receive a guest len-4 access — and SCCERR has wwr rows. Interesting — that's WHY only SCCERR has word rows! The address space is 0x40 window (relative_addr %= 0x40); guest wide accesses must be naturally aligned: len-2 at even offsets (all six even blocks reachable), len-4 at offsets ≡0 mod 4 within a block's arm — only SCCERR at 0x1c among guarded blocks. Hmm wait — what about a len-4 access at 0x00 (CHIPID)? Not a guarded block. What about len-4 at 0x3c? lands at... 0x3c arm — is there an arm at 0x3c? Not in the 12. So for the 12 guarded blocks, guest-reachable shapes: len 1 any, len 2 at the six even, len 4 at SCCERR only (0x1c), len 8? m88k st.d is two word stores? memory_rw len 8 — 0x1c % 8 ≠ 0, so no len-8 guest access to any guarded block (0x1c%8=4). Operator path: put w/put d at any address with warn. So `len == 2` at GENCTL: guest can't send len-4 to GENCTL; operator put w at 0x02 can — no row. So the mutant's reachable-landing channel is operator-only, unmeasured. SWEEP. But is it a REAL regression? The width guard's purpose: decline non-byte writes. `len == 2` declines halfwords but accepts words (operator-path). Real defect, unmeasured. Smallest edit: `len != 1` → `len == 2`, same size class as the measured `len > 2`.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:194:     But hold on — the measured shape 2 (`len > 2`) at SCCERR: under len>2, len-2 writes land at SCCERR (value-identical, invisible) but len-4... wait wwr sccerr dcl under M_LENGT2_SCCERR: len 4 > 2 → declined → wwr sccerr dcl stays 1; hwr sccerr dcl → len 2 not >2 → no announce → red ✓ (measured: hwr sccerr dcl red). ✓ consistent.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:196:     And the `len == 2` sweep — is it in scope for "clause-(d) enumeration"? The six shapes named in Q1(a) don't include it. Q2 explicitly wants shapes NOT in the tables. So my headline answer to Q2: `len != 1` → `len == 2` at any non-SCCERR arm (GENCTL is the juiciest — most-rowed arm) is a one-token-edit clean sweep; the detector has no len-4 row at 11 of 12 blocks (only SCCERR is 4-aligned, so only SCCERR can even receive a guest len-4 — but the OPERATOR path reaches any arm with put w, and the probe already uses the operator path for odd arms; it just never put w's). Wait, actually — does the probe ever do put w? "val w sccerr" and "wwr sccerr" — wwr sccerr might be guest st (word) at 0x1c. Anyway.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:200:     (viii) **IPL fall-through** (from earlier analysis): PSCALEADJ-class body (store nothing, print only), got no noig row. This is the strongest INTERNAL find: the repair closed the fall-through class at exactly the four arms PRETABLE §2 named, but §2's own table shows IPL's write body is ALSO print-only ("read-only ... ignored", write side). A fall-through at IPL: hwr ipl dcl stays 1, no state change, and the spurious "write to read-only PCCTWO_IPL; ignored" print is counted by NO row. 82/82 sweep. Wait — is that right? Let me double check the IPL arm: guard → decline → [break removed] → `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) { static int w; if (!w) { fatal("write to read-only PCCTWO_IPL; ignored"); w=1; } }`. Yes: prints once. No row counts "read-only PCCTWO_IPL" occurrences. The gate list has no "hwr ipl noig". So M_FALL_IPL = 82/82. AND it's a guest-reachable arm (even), so this isn't operator-only trivia — a guest st.h at IPL produces BOTH messages. Contract says declined writes don't reach the body. Same shape, same class as M_FALL_PSCALEADJ which was ruled a floor breach. The class closure MISSED IPL (and arguably VECBASE and SCCERR — check their bodies).
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:202:     What are VECBASE and SCCERR write bodies? Need the file. If VECBASE stores idata (plain store), a fall-through at VECBASE lands a byte — caught by... rb vecbase? rb vecbase's session writes wide to GENCTL, not VECBASE. Is there a session that writes wide to VECBASE and reads it back? op vecbase: put h at VECBASE + survival + dcl. NO readback after. So if VECBASE's body stores, M_FALL_VECBASE lands 0xbb (or whatever) into VECBASE — no row reads VECBASE after a wide write TO VECBASE → sweep! Wait — but PRETABLE §2 claims VECBASE readback = "rb vecbase". If rb vecbase doesn't put h at VECBASE, that claim is FALSE for the fall-through shape. rb vecbase (from the old 60): "VECBASE unchanged too -- the second lane of the declined halfword" — the session: setup(r5="0x50", r6="0xaabb") + program [ST_B(5, VECBASE)?, ST_H(6, GENCTL), LD_BU(4, VECBASE)]. So it byte-sets VECBASE=0x50, halfword-writes GENCTL (declined), reads VECBASE expecting 0x50 — checks the GENCTL decline didn't bleed into VECBASE's lane. It never writes wide to VECBASE. So a fall-through AT VECBASE (put h at 0x03 lands 0xbb into VECBASE) is unseen by rb vecbase (different session). M_FALL_VECBASE: op vecbase dcl stays 1 ✓, op vecbase exit0 ✓, rb vecbase's session untouched by the mutant? The mutant changes VECBASE's arm: in rb vecbase's session, the wide write is to GENCTL (its own arm, unmutated) — VECBASE read back 0x50 ✓ green. So M_FALL_VECBASE = 82/82 sweep IF VECBASE's body stores something. MUST CHECK the file. Similarly SCCERR: if body stores 0 always (value-identical), fall-through lands 0 over 0 — invisible but vacuous; unless it also reasserts or has other effects.
