regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:275:     The width-decline is per-instance (d->warned), but the READ-ONLY refusal below it still uses `static int w` — process-global. The comment says "Now per-instance, write-only, like the ten blocks above" — that sentence describes the WIDTH guard conversion (which IS per-instance via d->warned), but a reader could think the whole arm is per-instance. The read-side "IPL == 0 on read" static remains process-global. Is that a defect? It's a warn-once diagnostic, pre-existing (#119), not part of this repair's scope. The comment's "Now per-instance, write-only" is ambiguous but defensible (refers to the width latch). Minor wording wrinkle for Q4. Actually — the comment literally says the #119 static "said 'ignored' about a READ the memcpy above had already served. Now per-instance, write-only" — implying the read-side "ignored" was removed and what remains is write-only + per-instance. But the code still has a read-side branch (IPL == 0 on read, static w) AND the write-side static w. So the comment OVERSTATES: two function-statics remain. The width latch is per-instance; the #119 remnants are not. For Q4 honesty: flag it. It's comment-vs-code mismatch in the SHIPPED product diff region (though the IPL arm code itself isn't in the diff — the diff is comments + typedef only; the IPL arm was changed in the earlier round b78bb0f). Still, the brief inlines it as "the CURRENT file" and premise says comments were corrected. Worth a flag, small.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:344:  7. **SCCERR write body**: `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr] = 0; /* clear */` (line 549). Value-identical ONLY IF the register was already 0. Is it? Init memset zeroes everything, and nothing else writes SCCERR (grep: only line 549 writes it). So SCCERR is always 0 → clearing is vacuous → "VALUE-IDENTICAL (always 0)" ✓. But careful: a fall-through at SCCERR stores 0 — same as the fix's decline (which leaves it 0). Vacuous ✓. No reassert in SCCERR arm ✓.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:350:     IMPORTANT for M_FALL_IPL: fall-through deletes the `break` at line 620. Then a wide write: decline announces (bit IPL), then `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` → static w → prints "write to read-only PCCTWO_IPL; ignored" once. No store. Effects: spurious extra message. No row counts it. hwr ipl dcl counts declines = 1 ✓ green. So M_FALL_IPL = 82/82 — REAL sweep, guest-reachable (even offset 0x3e). The class closure covered PSCALEADJ's print-only body but not IPL's print-only body. CONFIRMED as a live cell... but wait — is it? Let me double-check the "hwr ipl" session: does the probe's hwr ipl session count anything about "ignored"? From the diff: rows are hwr ipl (exit0), hwr ipl lv (sentinel), hwr ipl dcl (DECL count 1). No "ignored" count. ✓ sweep confirmed.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:388:  Loop over 5 even blocks (genctl, t2ctl, t2icr, sccerr, scctx) × 4 rows (hrd, hrd lv, hwr, hwr lv) = 20 → cumulative 24
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:411:     - op vecbase session: `put h 0xfff42003, 0x0303` → decline announces (op vecbase dcl = 1 ✓ green), then falls through: stores (uint8_t)0x0303 & 0xf0 = 0x03 & 0xf0 = 0x00 into VECBASE (init 0x0f → becomes 0x00), HUH: 0x0303 & ~0xf0 ≠ 0 → fatal HUH (returns). op vecbase = exit0 ✓ green.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:419:  C. **M_LENEQ2 (`len != 1` → `len == 2`)**: at GENCTL: all st.h rows still declined (rb genctl, rb genctl lo, latch all/lv, latch same, latch two — all use len-2 → still declined ✓ green). len-4 writes: only wwr sccerr does a word write, at SCCERR only. At GENCTL, a guest st (word) at 0x02 would be alignment-faulted (0x02 % 4 ≠ 0) — but the OPERATOR `put w 0xfff42002` warns and stores len 4 → lands under the mutant. No row does that. So green. CONFIRMED sweep at 11 of 12 arms (all but SCCERR, which has wwr rows). Note: is it really true that only SCCERR is 4-aligned? Even guarded blocks: GENCTL 0x02, TxCTL 0x16 (0x16%4=2), TxICR 0x1a (%4=2), SCCERR 0x1c (%4=0 ✓), SCCTX 0x1e (%4=2), IPL 0x3e (%4=2). Yes — SCCERR is the only 4-aligned guarded block. The probe comment says exactly that (line 215). So a `len == 2` mutant at any other arm has no guest-reachable len-4 channel and no operator len-4 row. SWEEP.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:423:     Actually also — would `len == 2` red "latch all"? latch all uses put h (len 2) → declined ✓ 12 announces ✓ green. Yes, sweep.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:425:  D. **writeflag-drop (`len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` → `len != 1`)**: at an even arm, e.g. GENCTL: hrd genctl does LD_H len-2 READ → guard trips → decline announces "unimplemented len=2 write to PCCTWO_GENCTL -- declined" (a LIE during a read) + break. The read was already served by memcpy (line 415-417) BEFORE the switch → LD_H returns correct data. hrd genctl checks exit0 ✓; hrd genctl lv checks sentinel 0x5a5a5a5a from I_LD_SCR (r4 clobbered by LD_H then overwritten by I_LD_SCR? wait — program is [I_ST_SCR, LD_H(4, off), I_LD_SCR]: I_LD_SCR loads r4 from scratch — which I_ST_SCR stored as r5=0x5a5a5a5a. So r4 = 0x5a5a5a5a regardless of what LD_H read ✓). So the read VALUE is never checked in survival rows... EXCEPT val h genctl / val w sccerr, which use LD_HU/LD_W at even blocks! val h genctl: LD_HU(4, GENCTL) len 2 → under the writeflag-drop at GENCTL, the guard trips ON THE READ → announce + break → but the memcpy already served the read → r4 = 0x8250 ✓ green! The decline doesn't change the read value. But it PRINTS a decline message: does any row count DECL in the val h genctl session? No — val h genctl only checks the printed r4. Green. Damn. So M_NOWRITEFLAG at GENCTL sweeps too.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:427:     Wait — but does the extra announce corrupt any COUNT row? latch all is a different session. rb genctl dcl counts in rb genctl's session (which has no wide reads). acc genctl nod counts DECL=0 in the acc session — the acc session is ST_B + LD_BU (both len 1) → no trip → green. So yes: M_NOWRITEFLAG_GENCTL = 82/82. The only evidence is a spurious "declined" message during hrd/val sessions that no row counts. CONFIRMED by inspection.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:429:     Note the contract says "READS ACCEPT" — this mutant makes reads DECLINE (announce), which violates the shipped contract's own words, and nothing sees it because (a) read value unaffected (memcpy above), (b) no row counts DECL during a read session. This is a genuine detector hole in the ACCEPT direction. It's arguably above the floor (clause (e) territory: the contract claims "READS ACCEPT" as an observable, and no row pins the absence of a read-decline). Actually — contract CATCHES says "the value a wide READ composes -- lane order and width -- not merely survival" — val h genctl pins the VALUE, and the value is unaffected by the mutant because memcpy serves reads before the switch. So the mutant is unobservable to value rows too. The detector's contract claims to catch wide-read lane issues, and it does; but it does NOT catch a read being declined (announced) — the announce is invisible.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:431:     Is this in-scope as a "floor breach"? The floor clause (d) = "guard kept, effect removed". This mutant's guard effect on WRITES is intact; it ADDS an effect on reads. So not clause (d). It's a contract-completeness gap: "reads ACCEPT" has no negative observable (no "no decline on read" row). The acc rows check "no decline message" for byte WRITES; no row checks "no decline message" for wide READS. Cheap fix: add `hrd genctl nod`-style rows (count DECL == 0 in the five hrd sessions). This is a legitimate finding for Q2: smallest edit = delete ` && writeflag == MEM_WRITE` (26 chars) at one arm; row that stays green: ALL of them; specifically the row one would expect to catch it — `hrd genctl lv` — checks only the sentinel, not the message count.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:437:     Hmm wait — actually there's a subtlety: `op pscale` (line 331-335) already does `put h` at PSCALEADJ with 0x0303 and counts DECL = 1. `op pscale noig` is a fresh session doing the same put h and counting "PSCALEADJ ignored" = 0. Under M_FALL_PSCALEADJ: decline announces AND the print happens → noig reds (measured: 3c shows exactly that row red ✓). ✓ real channel.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:442:  H. **M_KILL_IPL red signature includes hrd ipl**: under a kill at IPL on len!=1 — the mutant must have dropped writeflag or killed on any access. hrd ipl red means the mutant killed a READ. So M_KILL_IPL was `if (len != 1) exit(1)` (no writeflag) — interesting: that means the writeflag-drop shape WAS exercised as part of a kill mutant, but only in lethal form. The benign form (decline reads) sweeps.
