regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:841:  And one more verification for Q1(b): the `latch all` session — benign traffic setting a bit early. Each session is a fresh emulator (pty.fork + execv per session) → warned starts zeroed (memset in DEVINIT) ✓. Within the latch-all session, the 12 put h's are the only pcc2 writes. Each put h → one DEVICE_ACCESS → one decline → one bit. 12 distinct bits → 12 DECLs. Could the debugger's `put h` at an even address produce TWO memory_rw calls (two byte stores)? Premise 4 says ONE memory_rw(len 2) — measured pre-fix exit1 supports that (a single call reaching the arm). Also `put h` at even addresses: does the debugger warn only on MISALIGNED? Premise says warns at odd, stores anyway. At even addresses no warn. Either way one call. ✓.
regress\artifacts\pcc2widthkills\pass3_seats_20260903\kimi.txt:843:  One more Q1(b) sub-question: "Can two blocks announce on one bit and still count 12 (e.g. because the session touches a block twice)?" — the session touches each block once; if a pair shared a bit, count = 11 and no double-touch exists to push it back to 12. ✓ sound. And "can benign traffic set a bit early": within the session, the emulator boots (mvme187, -V = no... -V is? probably "no graphical..." whatever; -x). During boot with the dummy 4-byte file at 0x10000 and no OS running (the debugger is entered immediately? -x... In gxemul, -x starts the debugger? Actually the probe sends debugger commands, so the emulator must drop into the debugger. During machine init, does anything write pcc2? DEVINIT only memsets. No guest code runs before `step`. The timer tick runs on emulation — but ticks only run when the CPU runs; in the latch-all session there is no program/step at all, just put h commands → no ticks, no guest code. Even if a tick ran, ticks don't write d->warned (only pcc2_width_decline does, verified line 397 — the ONLY writer; grep "warned |=" count = 1 per PRETABLE §1; I saw the helper; DEVICE_ACCESS doesn't write warned elsewhere; there's a LOCAL `static int warned` at line 419 in the read path — different variable, shadows nothing, writes only itself. PRETABLE said "lines mentioning 'warned': 7" — let me count in the file: line 140 (struct), 397, 398, 419, 424, 425... that's 6ish. Whatever, close enough; the local static is a different variable sharing the NAME — actually that's worth a nit: PRETABLE §1 says "One writer, pcc2_width_decline(), sets exactly the bit its caller names; nothing in DEVICE_ACCESS or a tick writes the field" — TRUE for d->warned, but there IS a function-static local named `warned` in the read path (line 419) — an awk/grep for 'warned' would count it; PRETABLE's count "7 lines mentioning warned" presumably includes it. Fine, honest enough.
