tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31772-   "opened": "2026-09-07",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31773-   "title": "dev_lca.c DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): THREE defects in eleven lines -- an off-by-one guard that lets a bus-hitting read return all zeros, a discarded downstream failure result, and an uninitialised byte consumed on that failure",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-31774-   "witness": null,
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31775:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  The lane-semantics half was raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) with an\nenumerated table; the third defect I found by reading the function while checking that table.\n\n    ofs = relative_addr & 3;                       <- guest address bits 5-6\n    if (ofs > len) { fatal(...); exit(1); }        <- `>' where `>=' was meant\n    ...\n    uint8_t byte;                                  <- never initialised\n    cpu->memory_rw(..., &byte, 1, MEM_READ, ...);  <- result DISCARDED\n    for (i = 0; i < len; i++)\n            data[i] = i == ofs ? byte : 0x00;\n\n**1. OFF-BY-ONE.**  The guard is `>' rather than `>=', so ofs == len passes.  codex's table, re-derived:\n\n    len=1  ofs {0,1} accepted      len=4  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n    len=2  ofs {0,1,2} accepted    len=8  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n\nFor (len,ofs) = (1,1) and (2,2) the loop never reaches i == ofs, so **the access performs a real downstream\nread and returns ALL ZEROS**.  The guest is told the device answered zero when that lane was never asked.\n\n**2. THE READ PATH DISCARDS THE FAILURE RESULT** that the WRITE path returns -- codex's observation, and the\nasymmetry is visible with the two branches side by side.\n\n**3. `byte' IS UNINITIALISED and is consumed if that discarded read failed.**  This is the FOURTH instance\nof the uninitialised-consumption family recorded on 2026-09-07, alongside [[sgirebufuninit]],\n[[sgigbeconvover]] and #95's incomplete class closure.  **#95 zeroes device READ buffers, so a\ndevice-served read is covered; a read that FAILS before reaching a handler is not** -- the same gap\nsgirebufuninit turns on.\n\nMED rather than HIGH: the wrong-lane read is a correctness defect with no host death and nothing disclosed\nbut a stale stack byte.  The exit(1) in the same function is [[lcabadval]]'s range site, graded there.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability of the (1,1)/(2,2) pairs is established by reading; nothing shows a guest driving\nthem.\n\n*** codex ASSESS 2026-09-08.  I DELIBERATELY DID NOT TELL IT WHAT THE THREE DEFECTS WERE, and\nwarned it not to manufacture three.  It found SIX items and said the filed count is wrong. ***\n\n*\"Four behavioral issues, one conditional initialization gap, and one minor format mismatch.  **The\nfiled 'three' overstates the initialization claim.**\"*\n\n## *** THE TRAP: THE OBVIOUS ONE-CHARACTER FIX MAKES IT WORSE ***\n\n    dev_lca.c:193    if (ofs > len) {          <- equality PASSES\n\nOn equality the write selects byte zero via `ofs % len` and the read never places the downstream\nbyte anywhere -- **but the downstream read HAS OCCURRED and may have side effects.**  The guard\nshould be `ofs >= len`.\n\n**BUT the rejected-lane arm is `exit(1)` at :196, so changing only `>` to `>=` EXPANDS the\ntermination.**  *That is the same shape as `alphavmkill`, where deleting a kill would have published\na bogus 4 TB mapping: the boundary repair and the disposition repair are one design or neither.*\n\n**AND IT BOUNDED THE CLAIM RATHER THAN INFLATING IT:** *\"This is NOT a demonstrated buffer\noverrun\"* -- positive-length writes use modulo and the read loop stays within `len`; the modulo is\npart of the boundary behaviour, not an independent OOB.\n\n## THE IDIOM CENSUS -- five files, grepped rather than argued\n\n* **the same function's WRITE arm already returns the downstream result** -- the strongest precedent\n  for repairing its read arm;\n* both siblings (`lca_pci_conf`, `lca_ioc`) initialise `odata = 0`, and the config handler has a\n  non-terminating `dev = 0` fallback;\n* `eagle` and `footbridge_isa` initialise read results, and footbridge warns on unsupported writes\n  without exiting -- *but footbridge is not an equivalent sparse-lane mapper*;\n* `DEV_RAM_MIRROR` is the closest FORWARDING precedent: passes `PHYSICAL`, returns the nested result;\n* `wdc` -- ISA-connected -- already declines and latches for unsupported widths.\n* **no second implementation of LCA's sparse-lane packing exists in the fork.**\n\n## SIX ITEMS, each with disposition, bounded severity, and its observable\n\n    1 lane boundary        member 3   MED source-only   downstream access occurrence + payload\n                                                        (readback zero ALONE cannot distinguish)\n    2 process exit         member 3   MED pending witness -- and ALREADY RECORDED under lcabadval\n    3 lost read failure    member 7   LOW/documented    handler return status, writeback suppression\n    4 initialisation gap   member 7   LOW/documented    definedness analysis; normal readback is NOT\n                                                        an established channel\n    5 physical forwarding  member 7   MED conditional   which backing is accessed; bootstrap masks it\n    6 diagnostic format    member 7   LOW portability   no runtime text difference established\n\n**Member 5 FAULT is unavailable**, confirmed for the third time this session from a different file:\n`memory_rw.c`'s device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K arms and **no Alpha arm**, and\n`ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` is defined and unused.\n\n## *** THE DUPLICATE I ASKED IT TO LOOK FOR -- AND THERE IS ONE ***\n\n*\"The original `lcabadval` scope explicitly EXCLUDES range-guard analysis, while its later note\nrecords this exact termination site.  **Extract or reassign that site explicitly rather than counting\nit twice.**\"*  `lcaconfbus1kill` and `lcairqstubkill` remain separate; their severity and witnesses\ndo not transfer here.  **This is an open obligation on both rows and is not discharged by recording\nit here.**\n\n## SCOPE: SEVERAL ROUNDS\n\nBoundary + termination are **one** design (same guard site).  Read completion is another; physical\nforwarding another.  *\"Sharing a function -- or a prospective test executable -- does not establish\none semantic class and one oracle.\"*\n\n## NO WITNESS EXISTS AND NONE IS CHEAP\n\n*\"I found no in-tree regression exercising the sparse `lca_isa` handler.\"*  The config probes cover\nother handlers, and `lca_irqstub_probe.py` **accesses the downstream ISA mapping directly, bypassing\nthis shim.**  So every item here is a documented source candidate until someone drives the shim.",
